WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.620
&gt;&gt; Hello everyone. I
am Prashant Namekar.

00:00:07.621 --> 00:00:10.555
So, here I'm representing
Gaia Smart Cities.

00:00:10.556 --> 00:00:13.525
So, we are a small startup
in IoT space.

00:00:13.526 --> 00:00:16.539
So, what we are
doing in Microsoft,

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.965
we are trying to
develop something,

00:00:18.966 --> 00:00:22.545
speech recognition on
those small footprint devices.

00:00:22.546 --> 00:00:25.650
On the Edge itself, we'll
try to debug everything,

00:00:25.651 --> 00:00:27.765
and on the Edge itself,
we'll try to spot

00:00:27.766 --> 00:00:30.485
the keywords with
a low footprint ramps.

00:00:30.486 --> 00:00:34.875
So, here is what we have
proposed as an objective.

00:00:34.876 --> 00:00:37.220
So, the main objective
which we had

00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:40.100
is that we will be putting

00:00:40.101 --> 00:00:41.615
all the machine-learning codes

00:00:41.616 --> 00:00:43.805
on the small footprint
devices which has

00:00:43.806 --> 00:00:47.685
having extremely low RAM size,

00:00:47.686 --> 00:00:49.755
and apart from this,

00:00:49.756 --> 00:00:52.545
we are using
the audio input mics.

00:00:52.546 --> 00:00:55.075
So, we have to take
the inputs from the mic,

00:00:55.076 --> 00:00:56.190
we have to process that,

00:00:56.191 --> 00:00:58.805
we have to put that into the RAM.

00:00:58.806 --> 00:00:59.870
All the processing should

00:00:59.871 --> 00:01:02.140
happen in less than
100 milliseconds.

00:01:02.141 --> 00:01:04.555
So, we will need
a fast computing.

00:01:04.556 --> 00:01:06.680
It means the model side should be

00:01:06.681 --> 00:01:09.135
less of the machine learning's.

00:01:09.136 --> 00:01:10.580
So, other than that,

00:01:10.581 --> 00:01:13.515
we will reduce the number
of computations required.

00:01:13.516 --> 00:01:17.165
Also, we will be focusing
on the accuracy,

00:01:17.166 --> 00:01:20.495
what we can get and what
we can achieve more.

00:01:20.496 --> 00:01:22.475
So, this where we plan.

00:01:22.476 --> 00:01:25.550
What are the tangible outcomes
we got in one month of

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:28.780
time are: we have development
one generic tool.

00:01:28.781 --> 00:01:30.270
What the tool do is,

00:01:30.271 --> 00:01:32.270
you just have to
pass the checkpoints

00:01:32.271 --> 00:01:34.690
file like the TensorFlow
Model file 2A.

00:01:34.691 --> 00:01:36.830
It will just create

00:01:36.831 --> 00:01:39.465
a Microcontroller
independent libraries.

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:42.745
That is.h files, that
is C library files.

00:01:42.746 --> 00:01:45.660
You can directly put that onto
the Microcontroller thing.

00:01:45.661 --> 00:01:47.740
So, other than that,

00:01:47.741 --> 00:01:50.510
we have developed
our own machine learning

00:01:50.511 --> 00:01:54.615
libraries for the Cortex
M4 and Cortex M7,

00:01:54.616 --> 00:01:59.730
for the LSTM and the FastGRNN
and also for GRU thing.

00:01:59.731 --> 00:02:02.180
So, in one month of time we have

00:02:02.181 --> 00:02:04.515
almost 60 competitive
experiments on

00:02:04.516 --> 00:02:07.460
different Recurrent
Neural Network models.

00:02:07.461 --> 00:02:09.395
Other than that, we have

00:02:09.396 --> 00:02:11.660
Android app to
gather the dataset.

00:02:11.661 --> 00:02:13.460
So, as currently we are using

00:02:13.461 --> 00:02:16.825
the Google Seven and Google
30 dataset for our models,

00:02:16.826 --> 00:02:19.900
we will be moving to some
of the Indian languages.

00:02:19.901 --> 00:02:23.120
So, we have worked on

00:02:23.121 --> 00:02:26.155
the different ML models
are the DSCNN,

00:02:26.156 --> 00:02:31.190
GRU, LSTM, FastGRNN and
FastGRNN with Low Rank.

00:02:31.191 --> 00:02:32.600
So, this is
the Microcontroller we

00:02:32.601 --> 00:02:34.640
will be using for
our development purpose.

00:02:34.641 --> 00:02:38.180
The RAM sizes I spoke is 128 KB

00:02:38.181 --> 00:02:41.515
of RAM and 512 KB of Flash.

00:02:41.516 --> 00:02:45.520
It is operating at
a CPU speed of 180 MHz

00:02:45.521 --> 00:02:50.810
and same thing we have developed
for M4 also and M7 also.

00:02:50.811 --> 00:02:55.960
The main reason of using
the M4 and M7 was,

00:02:55.961 --> 00:02:58.760
it is having an inbuilt DSP
instructions support and

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:01.620
some of the Neural Network
Libraries are supported.

00:03:01.621 --> 00:03:03.750
So, for the DSP Library
we are using for

00:03:03.751 --> 00:03:06.610
the dot product and
the dot products addition,

00:03:06.611 --> 00:03:10.585
subtraction, vector copy
and some of the FFT things.

00:03:10.586 --> 00:03:13.910
Also, we are storing
the wavefiles in the.h format

00:03:13.911 --> 00:03:17.065
for the offline training and all,

00:03:17.066 --> 00:03:18.730
and we will be saving

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:22.175
the hamming frames and
all in the.h file itself.

00:03:22.176 --> 00:03:26.045
So, I'll be passing
the mic to Sneha.

00:03:26.046 --> 00:03:28.180
&gt;&gt; Next, I will be
taking you all to

00:03:28.181 --> 00:03:30.430
the workflow of Keyword spotting.

00:03:30.431 --> 00:03:34.605
So basically, the data
which we have is audio.

00:03:34.606 --> 00:03:36.730
So, we cannot directly
send the audio

00:03:36.731 --> 00:03:39.180
to the Neural Network
to get the predictions.

00:03:39.181 --> 00:03:40.420
So, first of all, we need to

00:03:40.421 --> 00:03:42.855
extract some features
out of the audio.

00:03:42.856 --> 00:03:44.400
Here we are doing that,

00:03:44.401 --> 00:03:48.195
you can see there are
Speech Signals there.

00:03:48.196 --> 00:03:51.310
We are processing them to
get this Speech Features and

00:03:51.311 --> 00:03:54.520
then putting those features
into the Neural Network.

00:03:54.521 --> 00:03:56.230
The Neural Network does gives us

00:03:56.231 --> 00:03:59.230
the predictions of the audio.

00:03:59.231 --> 00:04:02.155
For this problem set,

00:04:02.156 --> 00:04:05.100
we have used the dataset
which was given by Google,

00:04:05.101 --> 00:04:06.910
that is called Google
speech commands,

00:04:06.911 --> 00:04:10.520
and it has 30 keywords
like yes, no.

00:04:10.521 --> 00:04:15.000
It has a background noise
files also with it.

00:04:15.001 --> 00:04:16.970
We have trained the model for

00:04:16.971 --> 00:04:19.980
30 class and seven class problem.

00:04:19.981 --> 00:04:23.260
Also, the train,
test and validation

00:04:23.261 --> 00:04:27.500
set split was given in
the dataset itself.

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:31.455
So, the features
we have extracted

00:04:31.456 --> 00:04:35.815
are logarithm of
the mel scale filter banks.

00:04:35.816 --> 00:04:39.100
These are extracted for
one second audio so we

00:04:39.101 --> 00:04:43.160
get a feature matrix
of dimension 99 X 32.

00:04:43.161 --> 00:04:46.030
The dataset is also
normalized with

00:04:46.031 --> 00:04:49.405
the mean and standard deviation
of the training dataset.

00:04:49.406 --> 00:04:53.580
Next, these are the models
that we are using,

00:04:53.581 --> 00:04:55.645
we are using basic RNN models.

00:04:55.646 --> 00:04:58.150
I'm sure you all are
well aware of what

00:04:58.151 --> 00:05:02.215
is RNN models so I will
not go that much into it.

00:05:02.216 --> 00:05:05.220
We're using LSTM and FastGRNN.

00:05:05.221 --> 00:05:09.640
These two are the
different versions of RNN.

00:05:09.641 --> 00:05:13.010
So, we have trained
three different models,

00:05:13.011 --> 00:05:16.605
LSTM, FastGRNN and
FastGRNN with low rank.

00:05:16.606 --> 00:05:17.890
Now, we have trained

00:05:17.891 --> 00:05:20.565
more than 60 models
with different classes:

00:05:20.566 --> 00:05:24.555
optimizers and hidden states,
learning rates.

00:05:24.556 --> 00:05:27.805
Here are some of our results
that you can see.

00:05:27.806 --> 00:05:30.750
We have the model:
Learning rate, Optimizers,

00:05:30.751 --> 00:05:33.240
Batch Size, Epochs, Weight Size,

00:05:33.241 --> 00:05:35.805
Accuracy and all these things.

00:05:35.806 --> 00:05:40.915
Then, after doing
all these experiments,

00:05:40.916 --> 00:05:43.540
we have finally
reached onto one of

00:05:43.541 --> 00:05:45.890
the models which we

00:05:45.891 --> 00:05:48.655
decided to be deployed
on the Microcontroller.

00:05:48.656 --> 00:05:50.870
The model deployed had

00:05:50.871 --> 00:05:53.720
the following
specifications: it was

00:05:53.721 --> 00:05:56.600
a seven Class model
with 16 Hidden States,

00:05:56.601 --> 00:06:01.480
Accuracy of 91.1% and
Weight Size of three 3.6 Kb,

00:06:01.481 --> 00:06:03.640
it was with the background noise

00:06:03.641 --> 00:06:06.370
and quantized non-linearities.

00:06:06.371 --> 00:06:09.645
Next, I would like to
call upon Shubham.

00:06:09.646 --> 00:06:12.305
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Shubham.

00:06:12.306 --> 00:06:15.920
So, now we have trained
our models in TensorFlow.

00:06:15.921 --> 00:06:18.410
So, the important thing
that we want to do is,

00:06:18.411 --> 00:06:22.735
we want to get all these models
onto our Microcontroller.

00:06:22.736 --> 00:06:25.595
So, the first thing
that we need to do is,

00:06:25.596 --> 00:06:28.915
we need to somehow
convert the whole of

00:06:28.916 --> 00:06:32.020
the TensorFlow models
into a format that is

00:06:32.021 --> 00:06:36.090
recognized by C. So for
that particular purpose,

00:06:36.091 --> 00:06:42.085
we decided to create a tool
that will auto-generate

00:06:42.086 --> 00:06:45.295
these.h files for us
that will contain

00:06:45.296 --> 00:06:47.050
all the important information

00:06:47.051 --> 00:06:48.980
of the models like
the hidden weights,

00:06:48.981 --> 00:06:50.170
the input, the dense layer,

00:06:50.171 --> 00:06:53.360
the dense bias and
other information.

00:06:53.361 --> 00:06:56.485
Apart from that, we also
populated our.h file

00:06:56.486 --> 00:06:58.870
with some constants which

00:06:58.871 --> 00:07:00.835
are very critical for
audio processing,

00:07:00.836 --> 00:07:02.100
such as, the filter banks,

00:07:02.101 --> 00:07:03.570
hamming window et cetera.

00:07:03.571 --> 00:07:07.430
So, something that we

00:07:07.431 --> 00:07:15.210
observed during the course
of the workshop was that,

00:07:15.211 --> 00:07:17.645
the filter bank matrix

00:07:17.646 --> 00:07:20.330
was found to be sparse
and in the sparsity

00:07:20.331 --> 00:07:22.895
we observed a very unique pattern

00:07:22.896 --> 00:07:25.965
that actually allowed
us to exploit it.

00:07:25.966 --> 00:07:29.100
So, the pattern that we observed
was something like this.

00:07:29.101 --> 00:07:32.155
So initially, there
were some zeros,

00:07:32.156 --> 00:07:34.555
then there were some
non-zero elements and then

00:07:34.556 --> 00:07:37.150
there were more zeros.

00:07:37.151 --> 00:07:39.530
So, what is actually
happening was,

00:07:39.531 --> 00:07:43.015
we had a continuous length
of non-zero elements,

00:07:43.016 --> 00:07:46.930
and which were preceded
and succeeded by zeros.

00:07:46.931 --> 00:07:51.220
So now, this kind of
observation made us think,

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.410
let us see how many
non-zero elements

00:07:55.411 --> 00:07:56.945
we have in this whole matrix.

00:07:56.946 --> 00:08:00.005
So, the results that we
found quite surprising.

00:08:00.006 --> 00:08:02.050
So, we used, as you can see that

00:08:02.051 --> 00:08:05.680
the entire matrix is 32 x 257,

00:08:05.681 --> 00:08:07.290
which turns out to be around

00:08:07.291 --> 00:08:10.385
8,224 total number of
elements in the matrix.

00:08:10.386 --> 00:08:11.985
But in the whole matrix,

00:08:11.986 --> 00:08:15.835
we found that there were
only 458 non-zero elements.

00:08:15.836 --> 00:08:18.985
Now, that got us to thinking,

00:08:18.986 --> 00:08:22.045
if we are multiplying this
matrix with another matrix of

00:08:22.046 --> 00:08:25.680
257 then we have to perform,

00:08:25.681 --> 00:08:26.950
for the whole matrix you have to

00:08:26.951 --> 00:08:29.695
perform around
8,224 computations.

00:08:29.696 --> 00:08:32.140
Instead why and that was majorly

00:08:32.141 --> 00:08:35.365
because we were doing a lot
of zero multiplications.

00:08:35.366 --> 00:08:38.190
So, instead of that we said,

00:08:38.191 --> 00:08:41.590
why can't we represent
this in another format and

00:08:41.591 --> 00:08:43.900
do only 458 multiplications

00:08:43.901 --> 00:08:46.395
corresponding to
the non-zero elements.

00:08:46.396 --> 00:08:51.850
Part of it, after thinking
about how we could do this,

00:08:51.851 --> 00:08:54.465
we came up with this
particular representation.

00:08:54.466 --> 00:08:57.130
That we could just
store the length of

00:08:57.131 --> 00:09:00.410
the non-zero elements
of each row,

00:09:00.411 --> 00:09:03.185
the column index at which
the non-zero elements start and

00:09:03.186 --> 00:09:05.055
all the non-zero elements as

00:09:05.056 --> 00:09:07.210
a single array of 458 elements.

00:09:07.211 --> 00:09:09.720
So initially, as you can see we

00:09:09.721 --> 00:09:16.955
were storing 8,224 elements
for the filter banks.

00:09:16.956 --> 00:09:19.490
But, with this kind
of representation we

00:09:19.491 --> 00:09:22.755
just have around 500 elements,

00:09:22.756 --> 00:09:25.045
500 floating point numbers.

00:09:25.046 --> 00:09:29.205
So, an estimate of the size
is like the first model,

00:09:29.206 --> 00:09:32.740
has each floating point
number is four bytes,

00:09:32.741 --> 00:09:35.550
it would have occupied
32 KB of flash.

00:09:35.551 --> 00:09:38.105
But, with this kind
of representation,

00:09:38.106 --> 00:09:42.120
we occupied only to 2 KB
of flash and secondly,

00:09:42.121 --> 00:09:44.240
the computations
much more faster.

00:09:44.241 --> 00:09:46.070
Indeed, this proved
out to be very

00:09:46.071 --> 00:09:47.675
beneficial to us because

00:09:47.676 --> 00:09:51.155
we were using this multiplication
every 10 milliseconds.

00:09:51.156 --> 00:09:52.850
So, an estimate of

00:09:52.851 --> 00:09:56.070
how much time got
decrease was initially,

00:09:56.071 --> 00:09:59.240
a model that we had
done without using

00:09:59.241 --> 00:10:02.240
any of these optimizations

00:10:02.241 --> 00:10:05.375
took around 1.58
seconds in LSTM model,

00:10:05.376 --> 00:10:09.055
but when we shifted
to a FastGRNN model,

00:10:09.056 --> 00:10:11.600
and we performed
this optimization

00:10:11.601 --> 00:10:13.465
and several other optimizations,

00:10:13.466 --> 00:10:17.360
we brought down the time
to 89 milliseconds.

00:10:17.361 --> 00:10:19.580
So, there was a huge gain for

00:10:19.581 --> 00:10:23.040
us by using this kind of
representation of metrics.

00:10:23.041 --> 00:10:27.320
So, I would like to hand over
the mic to Yash and he'll

00:10:27.321 --> 00:10:31.930
explain the whole pipeline
in summary. Yes. Thank you.

00:10:31.931 --> 00:10:34.700
&gt;&gt; Now, here we have
received the.h file,

00:10:34.701 --> 00:10:36.555
now we need to do
the predictions.

00:10:36.556 --> 00:10:38.330
In the Microcontroller,
we were having

00:10:38.331 --> 00:10:41.405
a DMA buffer of 20 milliseconds.

00:10:41.406 --> 00:10:44.000
It can store the audio sample

00:10:44.001 --> 00:10:45.620
of 20 millisecond over there.

00:10:45.621 --> 00:10:49.230
Now here, we have MEMS Microphone

00:10:49.231 --> 00:10:51.440
from where we get the PCM Values

00:10:51.441 --> 00:10:54.240
and we store it in
our DMA Buffer.

00:10:54.241 --> 00:10:56.705
Now, in this DMA buffer,

00:10:56.706 --> 00:10:58.865
when it feels
the first 10 millisecond,

00:10:58.866 --> 00:11:03.070
it gives it a Half Transfer
Callback and we give

00:11:03.071 --> 00:11:05.785
that 10 millisecond audio

00:11:05.786 --> 00:11:07.610
in our 30 millisecond buffer

00:11:07.611 --> 00:11:09.170
which is for feature extraction.

00:11:09.171 --> 00:11:11.060
We have made that 30
millisecond buffer

00:11:11.061 --> 00:11:13.480
because we need
25 millisecond of window,

00:11:13.481 --> 00:11:15.900
so as to extract the features.

00:11:15.901 --> 00:11:20.320
After that, when it passes
that 10 millisecond,

00:11:20.321 --> 00:11:23.020
it keeps on updating
this DMA buffer.

00:11:23.021 --> 00:11:27.730
While we transfer the data to
our 30 millisecond buffer,

00:11:27.731 --> 00:11:29.010
it keeps on updating

00:11:29.011 --> 00:11:31.585
the 10 to 20 milliseconds
in this DMA buffer.

00:11:31.586 --> 00:11:34.270
After completing
this standard 20 millisecond,

00:11:34.271 --> 00:11:35.930
it gives a Full Transfer

00:11:35.931 --> 00:11:38.290
Callback and I pass
it that time to

00:11:38.291 --> 00:11:42.670
20 millisecond over there
and similarly for 20 to 30.

00:11:42.671 --> 00:11:44.045
After I fill

00:11:44.046 --> 00:11:46.560
the thirty 30 millisecond
buffer window over there,

00:11:46.561 --> 00:11:49.300
I use the 25 millisecond window

00:11:49.301 --> 00:11:51.175
to extract the features and I get

00:11:51.176 --> 00:11:55.100
32 Log Mel Bank filter
and I initialize it and

00:11:55.101 --> 00:11:57.510
if the first FastGRNN layer

00:11:57.511 --> 00:12:00.070
with sixteen 16 hidden
units with zeros.

00:12:00.071 --> 00:12:03.055
This is the first time frame
of the FastGRNN layer

00:12:03.056 --> 00:12:05.640
since we are using zero
to 25 millisecond.

00:12:05.641 --> 00:12:08.630
This is the input vector

00:12:08.631 --> 00:12:11.780
and this is the previous
hidden search that we get.

00:12:11.781 --> 00:12:14.685
We compute the next hidden units

00:12:14.686 --> 00:12:17.820
for next FastGRNNGate and I

00:12:17.821 --> 00:12:21.070
copy those weights
over there and we

00:12:21.071 --> 00:12:24.800
need a 10 millisecond
straight over here.

00:12:24.801 --> 00:12:28.000
So, I shift that 30
millisecond buffer

00:12:28.001 --> 00:12:33.125
and the next 30 to 40
millisecond audio,

00:12:33.126 --> 00:12:35.345
we take it from the DMA buffer

00:12:35.346 --> 00:12:37.630
and again store it in
our 30 millisecond buffer,

00:12:37.631 --> 00:12:40.280
and again extract
the Mel Filter Bank and

00:12:40.281 --> 00:12:41.410
again do the same for

00:12:41.411 --> 00:12:43.480
the second time frame
of that earlier.

00:12:43.481 --> 00:12:47.790
We do it for 99 steps since
we have trained our model on

00:12:47.791 --> 00:12:50.165
one second audio which has

00:12:50.166 --> 00:12:54.350
99 frames of 25 millisecond
with 10 second straight.

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:57.060
Here, we are firing

00:12:57.061 --> 00:12:59.395
20 FastGRNN layers and

00:12:59.396 --> 00:13:01.440
computing the
predictions over here.

00:13:01.441 --> 00:13:04.290
I will show you
how it is working.

00:13:04.291 --> 00:13:07.480
If I do the two second
audio over there,

00:13:07.481 --> 00:13:09.745
I initialize the first
FastGRNN layer at

00:13:09.746 --> 00:13:11.890
the start and I compute

00:13:11.891 --> 00:13:16.405
that FastGRNN layer and I get
the prediction, like six.

00:13:16.406 --> 00:13:18.470
I've got the prediction maximum

00:13:18.471 --> 00:13:20.470
of the probabilities of
the soft max that we

00:13:20.471 --> 00:13:22.400
obtain after applying the

00:13:22.401 --> 00:13:25.540
densely and I store
it in a buffer.

00:13:25.541 --> 00:13:28.390
The buffer is called ten
previous predictions,

00:13:28.391 --> 00:13:30.930
like it will store
the previous ten predictions

00:13:30.931 --> 00:13:32.910
in that buffer over there.

00:13:32.911 --> 00:13:34.900
Here, I initialize

00:13:34.901 --> 00:13:38.435
the second FastGRNN layer
after 50 milliseconds.

00:13:38.436 --> 00:13:40.910
Like the first FastGRNN layer

00:13:40.911 --> 00:13:43.280
is from zero second
to one second.

00:13:43.281 --> 00:13:45.220
The second FastGRNN layer is from

00:13:45.221 --> 00:13:47.980
50 millisecond to
one second, 50 milliseconds.

00:13:47.981 --> 00:13:52.635
I again do the predictions
over here and I keep that data

00:13:52.636 --> 00:13:55.375
in my buffer which has

00:13:55.376 --> 00:13:57.500
20 previous predictions
and I do the

00:13:57.501 --> 00:14:01.330
same again and again and again.

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:11.145
Since here, we have five times
predicted three over here.

00:14:11.146 --> 00:14:13.960
So, it calculates the frequency,

00:14:13.961 --> 00:14:17.985
how many times a particular
keyword has been predicted.

00:14:17.986 --> 00:14:19.875
If it goes more than five times,

00:14:19.876 --> 00:14:22.485
then it predicts
that particular keyword

00:14:22.486 --> 00:14:24.830
and it shows output.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:31.440
We applied 20
FastGRNN layers over

00:14:31.441 --> 00:14:34.230
here like that 20 FastGRNN layer

00:14:34.231 --> 00:14:37.800
is starting just
before one second

00:14:37.801 --> 00:14:40.920
and ending just before
the second seconds.

00:14:40.921 --> 00:14:45.570
Sorry. When the one second
is completed,

00:14:45.571 --> 00:14:48.405
I again re-initialize
the first FastGRNN layer and I

00:14:48.406 --> 00:14:53.520
again started to compute
for the prediction.

00:14:53.521 --> 00:14:54.820
&gt;&gt; Hello. I'm Sheena Joy.

00:14:54.821 --> 00:14:57.300
So, as we already
discussed that we're

00:14:57.301 --> 00:14:59.910
converting the CKPT
files to.h files,

00:14:59.911 --> 00:15:01.460
so that they can be fed into

00:15:01.461 --> 00:15:02.950
the microcontroller and then

00:15:02.951 --> 00:15:04.505
used for the further processing.

00:15:04.506 --> 00:15:06.820
This is the tool that
will help us to do it.

00:15:06.821 --> 00:15:09.265
First, all that you
need to do is just

00:15:09.266 --> 00:15:11.085
upload detail checkpoint files

00:15:11.086 --> 00:15:12.190
and the corresponding mean,

00:15:12.191 --> 00:15:14.260
and the standard deviation files.

00:15:14.261 --> 00:15:16.630
So, in the background, these
files they get uploaded into

00:15:16.631 --> 00:15:19.380
the Azure block and
then the file starts

00:15:19.381 --> 00:15:22.620
executing which converts
those files into the.h

00:15:22.621 --> 00:15:27.215
file and it gets again uploaded
into another Azure block.

00:15:27.216 --> 00:15:28.955
So, once this process is done,

00:15:28.956 --> 00:15:30.790
you will be navigated
to another page,

00:15:30.791 --> 00:15:31.990
which in this space,

00:15:31.991 --> 00:15:33.310
by clicking the "Download".

00:15:33.311 --> 00:15:36.230
You would be allowed to download
the weights file that is

00:15:36.231 --> 00:15:37.650
generated and put in

00:15:37.651 --> 00:15:39.980
the Azure block to
your local system.

00:15:39.981 --> 00:15:42.660
From this, you can feed it
into the Microcontroller.

00:15:42.661 --> 00:15:45.060
The best part is this whole tool

00:15:45.061 --> 00:15:47.095
it can be used in
any Microcontroller,

00:15:47.096 --> 00:15:50.050
that is, it is independent
of any Microcontroller.

00:15:50.051 --> 00:15:51.900
That is because it uses

00:15:51.901 --> 00:15:54.845
the only basic
integer data types.

00:15:54.846 --> 00:15:58.945
So, you can use this tool for
any other Microcontroller.

00:15:58.946 --> 00:16:03.170
Right now, it works only
for the FastGRNN and LSTM,

00:16:03.171 --> 00:16:06.450
but again you can extend
it to any other model.

00:16:06.451 --> 00:16:08.775
So, in this three weeks,

00:16:08.776 --> 00:16:11.160
we had a chance as Nehal already

00:16:11.161 --> 00:16:13.645
mentioned to work with
around 60 models.

00:16:13.646 --> 00:16:16.060
We have trained the models
using the LSTM files,

00:16:16.061 --> 00:16:18.270
FastGRNN and FastGRNN-low rank.

00:16:18.271 --> 00:16:19.920
Each of which we've trained with

00:16:19.921 --> 00:16:22.300
the 30 class and
the seven class and

00:16:22.301 --> 00:16:24.345
again each of those
we've trained it

00:16:24.346 --> 00:16:26.740
with background noise and
without background noise.

00:16:26.741 --> 00:16:29.010
So, we've had some observations,

00:16:29.011 --> 00:16:30.390
some of them are pretty evident,

00:16:30.391 --> 00:16:32.430
that is the seven
class accuracies

00:16:32.431 --> 00:16:34.375
are better than the 30 class and

00:16:34.376 --> 00:16:37.250
the accuracies of
without background noise models

00:16:37.251 --> 00:16:39.460
are better than the
with background noise.

00:16:39.461 --> 00:16:43.450
So, other one is,
the FastGRNN-low rank model.

00:16:43.451 --> 00:16:45.875
For the higher number
of hidden states,

00:16:45.876 --> 00:16:48.880
that is 128 hidden states,
it worked very well.

00:16:48.881 --> 00:16:51.315
I mean, it gave us
a better accuracy,

00:16:51.316 --> 00:16:54.400
but when we were using it
for the 16 hidden states,

00:16:54.401 --> 00:16:56.650
it gave us a little
less accuracy.

00:16:56.651 --> 00:17:01.260
So, we had to narrow down
between the FastGRNN and LSTM.

00:17:01.261 --> 00:17:03.630
We were only keen on using

00:17:03.631 --> 00:17:07.380
the 16 hidden states with
background noise model.

00:17:07.381 --> 00:17:10.910
So, for the 30 class with
background noise model,

00:17:10.911 --> 00:17:14.010
FastGRNN has given us
the better accuracy,

00:17:14.011 --> 00:17:15.390
but when we were using

00:17:15.391 --> 00:17:18.215
the seven class with
background noise,

00:17:18.216 --> 00:17:21.550
the LSTM slightly given us
the better accuracies around

00:17:21.551 --> 00:17:25.080
maybe one percent higher or
less than one percent higher.

00:17:25.081 --> 00:17:27.510
But considering the model size,

00:17:27.511 --> 00:17:30.015
we had to choose
the FastGRNN model.

00:17:30.016 --> 00:17:33.050
The FastGRNN model is three
times smaller than that of

00:17:33.051 --> 00:17:34.780
the LSTM model because

00:17:34.781 --> 00:17:37.330
we have to fit this in
a constrained device,

00:17:37.331 --> 00:17:39.775
which was the FastGRNN model and

00:17:39.776 --> 00:17:42.515
we've used the quantized
non-linearity for it.

00:17:42.516 --> 00:17:45.120
So, finally we had
a model size of around

00:17:45.121 --> 00:17:49.275
3.6 KB with 91.1
percent accuracy.

00:17:49.276 --> 00:17:52.180
Apart from that, we had
some other observations,

00:17:52.181 --> 00:17:54.400
like initially we
used the Tanh and

00:17:54.401 --> 00:17:56.010
the sigmoid functions but while

00:17:56.011 --> 00:17:58.175
computing them on
the Microcontroller,

00:17:58.176 --> 00:18:00.590
it took lot of time
for the computations.

00:18:00.591 --> 00:18:03.970
But when we started using
the quantized non linearities

00:18:03.971 --> 00:18:06.095
because they use
only basic functions

00:18:06.096 --> 00:18:07.795
like minimum and maximum,

00:18:07.796 --> 00:18:11.505
the computation time decreased.

00:18:11.506 --> 00:18:13.920
While training the models,

00:18:13.921 --> 00:18:15.840
when we were using
the quantized ones,

00:18:15.841 --> 00:18:18.440
the Adam optimizer
worked well for us,

00:18:18.441 --> 00:18:21.650
and when we used
the normal user functions,

00:18:21.651 --> 00:18:24.185
the momentum optimizer
worked well.

00:18:24.186 --> 00:18:26.200
Also initially, we started using

00:18:26.201 --> 00:18:28.130
the log functions but because

00:18:28.131 --> 00:18:29.865
it takes the input
as the double and

00:18:29.866 --> 00:18:31.680
we were providing
it with the float,

00:18:31.681 --> 00:18:34.290
there was this type promotion
that was happening which

00:18:34.291 --> 00:18:37.010
took a lot of time
for the computations.

00:18:37.011 --> 00:18:39.315
But when we switched
to the Logf function,

00:18:39.316 --> 00:18:41.760
it helped us with
the computation time.

00:18:41.761 --> 00:18:43.890
Also, while training, we've

00:18:43.891 --> 00:18:45.570
noticed that after
certain epochs,

00:18:45.571 --> 00:18:47.600
the last spiked up.

00:18:47.601 --> 00:18:49.040
So, to overcome that,

00:18:49.041 --> 00:18:50.605
after certain number of epochs,

00:18:50.606 --> 00:18:52.950
we've halfened our learning rate.

00:18:52.951 --> 00:18:57.360
We've validated the Microsoft
FastGRNN model in real time

00:18:57.361 --> 00:19:02.285
on our Microcontroller which
has a single core processor.

00:19:02.286 --> 00:19:05.300
I would like to give
it to [inaudible].

00:19:05.301 --> 00:19:06.530
&gt;&gt; I mean, the motivation
that we built

00:19:06.531 --> 00:19:08.395
was extremely small footprint,

00:19:08.396 --> 00:19:12.780
low computation battery as
to last over six months,

00:19:12.781 --> 00:19:17.470
no connectivity and
what Alexa does,

00:19:17.471 --> 00:19:19.520
we wanted to do it
completely on the edge.

00:19:19.521 --> 00:19:21.780
Those are the motivations.
Then we realized that it

00:19:21.781 --> 00:19:25.145
could also solve these problems
of security of privacy,

00:19:25.146 --> 00:19:26.590
connectivity and power and

00:19:26.591 --> 00:19:28.390
that's what we've extended it to.

00:19:28.391 --> 00:19:30.550
In addition, the models
that we have built,

00:19:30.551 --> 00:19:31.775
the framework that we
have built is easily

00:19:31.776 --> 00:19:33.480
scalable across used cases.

00:19:33.481 --> 00:19:35.620
So, while today we're
doing it for 66 watts,

00:19:35.621 --> 00:19:36.875
we're doing it for

00:19:36.876 --> 00:19:38.840
a certain number of
one particular sector,

00:19:38.841 --> 00:19:41.925
we believe with
the framework that we have,

00:19:41.926 --> 00:19:44.895
it can replicate easily.

00:19:44.896 --> 00:19:47.780
I mean, in fact, that's this
so this the Microcontroller

00:19:47.781 --> 00:19:49.480
using as an ARM M4.

00:19:49.481 --> 00:19:50.860
It's a really small footprint.

00:19:50.861 --> 00:19:55.355
It's a 180 megahertz, but 256 KB.

00:19:55.356 --> 00:19:57.290
In that, way, the power
that it consume

00:19:57.291 --> 00:19:59.540
just 89 microamperes for

00:19:59.541 --> 00:20:02.215
every computation per
megahertz computation.

00:20:02.216 --> 00:20:03.720
Right now, with what
we have developed,

00:20:03.721 --> 00:20:05.730
we just need 89 milliseconds

00:20:05.731 --> 00:20:07.670
for one second speech analysis.

00:20:07.671 --> 00:20:09.280
Faster than you
speak. I think we can

00:20:09.281 --> 00:20:11.925
analyze behold what
you're trying to say.

00:20:11.926 --> 00:20:13.630
This is done at a 3.6 KB,

00:20:13.631 --> 00:20:15.510
as the team mentioned earlier.

00:20:15.511 --> 00:20:17.760
Without quantization,
when you quantize,

00:20:17.761 --> 00:20:20.840
it comes to less than 1.0 KB.

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:23.550
So, that's the ST 32 F4.

00:20:23.551 --> 00:20:25.700
This is the Azure IoT sphere.

00:20:25.701 --> 00:20:26.910
That's the master
controller which

00:20:26.911 --> 00:20:28.250
has about 500 megahertz,

00:20:28.251 --> 00:20:29.840
it's an ARM A7.

00:20:29.841 --> 00:20:33.535
It's far superior in terms
of computation power.

00:20:33.536 --> 00:20:34.700
We believe, if we increase

00:20:34.701 --> 00:20:36.295
the number of word size even to

00:20:36.296 --> 00:20:39.170
100 and if you don't quantize,

00:20:39.171 --> 00:20:45.035
we can actually get a model
size not more than 9.5 KB.

00:20:45.036 --> 00:20:47.380
This actually exceeds
the number of

00:20:47.381 --> 00:20:49.995
used cases that we can apply to.

00:20:49.996 --> 00:20:52.545
We're trying to extend it
to services and retail,

00:20:52.546 --> 00:20:53.880
hospitality services and

00:20:53.881 --> 00:20:55.255
industrial
manufacturing, as well.

00:20:55.256 --> 00:20:59.310
Pretty much, this doesn't
require connectivity or power.

00:20:59.311 --> 00:21:01.390
These are a number of
used cases that we believe

00:21:01.391 --> 00:21:03.235
we can move fast.

00:21:03.236 --> 00:21:06.025
Even panic detection
in cabs, feedback,

00:21:06.026 --> 00:21:08.840
even usage in cities,

00:21:08.841 --> 00:21:10.620
all of that is where we can.

00:21:10.621 --> 00:21:11.450
This one, I want to talk about.

00:21:11.451 --> 00:21:13.615
So, yesterday, I met this
[inaudible] from Intel and they have

00:21:13.616 --> 00:21:16.955
a smart home application which
they don't want it to be,

00:21:16.956 --> 00:21:18.615
they want it to be likely
connected to the internet.

00:21:18.616 --> 00:21:20.210
They want a lot processing
on the edge itself.

00:21:20.211 --> 00:21:22.960
They don't want,
for every analysis,

00:21:22.961 --> 00:21:25.280
like Alexa does, they don't
want to contact the server.

00:21:25.281 --> 00:21:26.730
They believe this can
be a good used case.

00:21:26.731 --> 00:21:28.235
Similarly in the connected car,

00:21:28.236 --> 00:21:29.875
I mean, just for starting a car,

00:21:29.876 --> 00:21:31.985
they want to move into
the start button and if

00:21:31.986 --> 00:21:34.120
you can give them
the ability to start this by

00:21:34.121 --> 00:21:36.050
saying talking and
the analysis and

00:21:36.051 --> 00:21:39.720
the voice print can be
analyzed on the edge,

00:21:39.721 --> 00:21:41.725
it solves a lot of problems
for a lot of people.

00:21:41.726 --> 00:21:43.330
Connectivity, security.

00:21:43.331 --> 00:21:44.485
What we are trying to do

00:21:44.486 --> 00:21:45.660
is increase the number
of languages.

00:21:45.661 --> 00:21:46.840
In fact, we built this model to

00:21:46.841 --> 00:21:49.225
actually replicate
across languages.

00:21:49.226 --> 00:21:51.190
We have to work on
false positives.

00:21:51.191 --> 00:21:53.650
We have been receiving
our false positives right now.

00:21:53.651 --> 00:21:56.670
We want to extend it
to more use cases.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:00.140
In fact, you will see
the demo that we have.

00:22:00.141 --> 00:22:02.280
It's a pretty small
footprint thing that power

00:22:02.281 --> 00:22:05.380
can go or acting on
a single AA battery.

00:22:05.381 --> 00:22:08.875
We think it can last
for about two months

00:22:08.876 --> 00:22:12.500
with at least 1,000
usage per day.

00:22:12.501 --> 00:22:14.440
We want to do beta trials.

00:22:14.441 --> 00:22:17.840
We also want to do,
adding a speaker to it.

00:22:17.841 --> 00:22:19.835
We have one model now, we're just

00:22:19.836 --> 00:22:21.030
doing speech recognition,

00:22:21.031 --> 00:22:23.435
we would like to do gender
detection, sentiment analysis.

00:22:23.436 --> 00:22:28.330
Also, the footprint
shouldn't exceed so much.

00:22:28.410 --> 00:22:32.455
I'll end with this, and,
well, without ending,

00:22:32.456 --> 00:22:36.280
I can't do it without
thanking the Microsoft team.

